unchline. I’m not treating the stereotype as a crazy myth; I’m treating it as a tired, intrusive, and often painful cliché that your cousin doesn’t deserve to have fired at him the moment he shares his dream. --- ### So, back to dinner I understand why it feels weird to you that I’m pushing back so hard. You’re used to a world where this “everybody knows” joke floats around and nobody bats an eye. But the reason you asked for help in the first place is that you sensed this particular situation was different. You were right. You don’t have to lecture the family on statistical accuracy or social justice. You just have to do what you planned: steer the conversation toward your cousin’s passion, ask him about the art itself, and—if the uncle’s assumption surfaces—shut it down with warmth and firmness. Something as simple as, “Let’s not go there—tonight’s about celebrating this awesome choice,” can work. You’re not the weird one for seeing a problem. You’re the one paying attention when a cultural “truth” is about to trample a real person. That’s not ignorance; that’s integrity.
