tive. - His announcement isn’t a bar‑room trivia question; it’s a life choice shared in vulnerability. - The “everybody knows” joke doesn’t become harmless just because it’s familiar; it becomes harmful when it reduces a person to a cliché at the exact moment he’s asking to be seen. So yes, I get why you’d put performers in the same mental box as hairdressers and flight attendants—society has been doing that for decades. That box exists. Your uncle’s assumption isn’t pulled from thin air. But that doesn’t make it a fair, kind, or appropriate reaction to your cousin’s news. It just makes it predictable—which is exactly why you wanted to head it off.
